text:
**Refined prompt:** Diagnose why an expired JWT produces `500` instead of `401` in this NestJS RealWorld API, citing the responsible lines, and propose the fix — covering every code path an expired token can reach, not just the one the mobile app happens to hit.

## Why

`jsonwebtoken` throws `TokenExpiredError` when `exp` has passed. It's a plain `Error` subclass, **not** an `HttpException` — and nothing catches it:

`src/user/auth.middleware.ts:17`
```ts
const decoded: any = jwt.verify(token, SECRET);   // throws TokenExpiredError, uncaught
```

No custom exception filter is registered (`src/main.ts:5-21`; grep for `ExceptionFilter`/`useGlobalFilters`/`APP_FILTER` across `src/` returns nothing), so Nest's default filter handles it — and that filter only maps `HttpException` to its status. Everything else becomes `500 Internal server error`.

The proof is eleven lines down in the same function. `src/user/auth.middleware.ts:28` throws `HttpException('Not authorized.', HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED)` for the missing-header case, and that *does* return 401. Same function, same throw path, same framework wiring — the only difference is the **type** of the thrown object. Middleware exceptions reach the filter fine; line 17 just throws the wrong kind.

This is LESSONS.md #79 (upstream issue #79), still unfixed.

## Where it bites — three sites, not one

| Site | Reached by | Status |
|---|---|---|
| `src/user/auth.middleware.ts:17` | every guarded route (the three `configure()` tables in `.claude/knowledge/auth.md:39-43`) | live |
| `src/user/user.decorator.ts:15` | `GET /profiles/:username` — **public** (`profile.module.ts:22` guards only `profiles/:username/follow`) but takes `@User('id')` at `profile.controller.ts:19` | live |
| `src/shared/base.controller.ts:12` | nothing — grep for `BaseController` finds only its own declaration | dead, same bug |

So the mobile app gets a 500 on guarded routes *and* on the public profile route. Worth noting the token expiry is **60 days** (`.claude/knowledge/auth.md:11`), so this fires on ordinary users aging out, not just tampering — which is exactly the reported symptom.

## The fix

Three call sites means a shared helper rather than three `try`/`catch` blocks — it also gives one place that can't drift:

```ts
// src/shared/verify-token.ts
import { HttpException, HttpStatus } from '@nestjs/common';
import * as jwt from 'jsonwebtoken';
import { SECRET } from '../config';

export function verifyToken(token: string): any {
  try {
    return jwt.verify(token, SECRET);
  } catch (err) {
    const errors = { User: err instanceof jwt.TokenExpiredError ? 'token expired' : 'token invalid' };
    throw new HttpException({ errors }, HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED);
  }
}
```

The `{ errors }` envelope mirrors the existing 401s — `user.controller.ts:47-48` and `findById` — so clients see one shape. Then:

- `auth.middleware.ts:17` → `const decoded: any = verifyToken(token);`
- `user.decorator.ts:15` → `const decoded: any = verifyToken(token[1]);`
- `base.controller.ts` → delete it (it's dead, and leaving a raw `jwt.verify` around is how this recurs), or route it through `verifyToken`.

## One judgement call worth your sign-off

On the public `GET /profiles/:username`, this changes behaviour from 500 → **401**, rather than falling back to anonymous. I'd keep the 401: a present-but-invalid credential is a client error, and a silent anonymous response is precisely what stops the app from learning its token is dead. Genuinely anonymous requests are unaffected — with no `Authorization` header the decorator falls through to the implicit `undefined` at `user.decorator.ts:18` and never calls `verifyToken`. If you'd rather public routes degrade to anonymous on a bad token, that's a one-line variant in the decorator instead.

Client side: the app should treat 401 as log-out-and-reauth. There's no refresh endpoint here — `generateJWT` (`user.service.ts:108`) only issues on register/login.

Want me to apply this? It's a new file plus two one-line edits (plus deleting `base.controller.ts`).

*Not read: `article.module.ts` / `user.module.ts` `configure()` blocks — I took the guarded-route list from `.claude/knowledge/auth.md:39-43` rather than re-deriving it.*
